# rules: swift-webview-javascript-bridge
# languages: swift
# fixture: swift/CWE-749.swift
# from: CWE-749 (Exposed Dangerous Method or Function), MASVS-PLATFORM-2
#
# `add(_:name:)` on a `WKUserContentController` installs a native handler that
# any script in the web view can call, as
# `window.webkit.messageHandlers.<name>.postMessage(...)`. "Any script" is the
# whole of the problem: it includes a third-party iframe, an ad, and whatever
# the page redirects to. So the handler runs on behalf of whoever wrote the
# page that happens to be loaded, and the app has no way to ask which.
#
# It is the same weakness as Android's `addJavascriptInterface`, and the same
# exclusion applies for the same reason. A web view showing HTML the app
# shipped can only run script the app shipped, so a method that mentions
# `Bundle.main` or `loadFileURL` is left alone.
#
# That test is the method's own text, which means a method that loads a bundled
# page *and* a remote one goes unreported too - coarse, and coarse in the quiet
# direction, exactly as `kotlin-webview-javascript-interface` is about
# `file:///android_asset/`.
#
# Both receiver spellings are one pattern because the receiver hole is written
# `$$$C`. A single hole there matches only a receiver the grammar read as a
# `user_type`, which is a bare name and nothing else - so
# `view.configuration.userContentController.add(...)`, which is how half of
# real code spells it, would be invisible. See GUIDE.md.

["$$$C.add($H, name: $N)"] as $bridges
| ["func $F() { $$$BODY }"] as $methods
| "Bundle\\.main|loadFileURL|loadHTMLString" as $showsItsOwnPage
| scan_ast("*.swift"; $bridges + $methods) as $all
| ( $all | of($bridges)
    | within($all | of($methods) | where_capture_not("BODY"; $showsItsOwnPage)) )
| finding("swift-webview-javascript-bridge";
    "every frame loaded in this web view can call $N, including one the app "
    + "did not choose; keep the bridge for content the app ships")
| report
